# Replacement narration also removes dangling textbook references, corrects
# oversimplifications, and connects retained visual explanations to this story.
OVERRIDES = {
    ("s01_views", 6): "A vector can be pictured as an arrow. Read the list three, minus one as a movement instruction: three steps right, then one step down. The arrow from where you started to where you finish records that displacement. The numbers and the arrow describe the same move.",
    ("s05_independence", 22): "Some vector is redundant; that does not mean every vector can be discarded. In the list one, zero; zero, one; and zero, two, the last two directions can replace each other. But remove one, zero and you lose every move with a nonzero horizontal component. Redundancy belongs to the list, with a particular relation showing which vectors participate.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 22): "The span contains zero: choose every coefficient to be zero. Add two recipes and you add their coefficients, producing another recipe. Scale a recipe and you scale its coefficients. Neither operation can escape the span, so the span is a subspace.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 26): "Zero belongs to both subspaces. If two vectors belong to both, their sum belongs to each subspace and hence to the intersection. The same holds for a scaled vector. The shared set therefore keeps all three properties: zero, closure under addition, and closure under scaling.",
    ("s02_axioms", 21): "For example, scaling a polynomial by a plus b is the same as adding a times that polynomial to b times that polynomial. Check any coefficient: ordinary distributivity of real numbers makes the two sides equal. The abstract rule is just the familiar numerical rule applied consistently to every coefficient.",
    ("s09_sums", 26): "Now take the plane z equals zero and the plane x equals zero. They share the y axis. Their sum has dimension two plus two minus one, which is three. Together the planes generate all three-dimensional space, but the shared direction gives us freedom in how we divide a destination between them.",
    ("s12_affine", 12): "Add these two location vectors and the result is one, one. Its coordinates add to two rather than one, so it lies on a different parallel line. The translated set is not closed under addition. The sum has counted the anchor twice.",
    ("s12_affine", 13): "Now take their midpoint. Half of each point gives one half, one half. Those coordinates add to one, so the midpoint stays on the original line. The two half-sized copies of the anchor have combined into exactly one copy.",
    ("s12_affine", 16): "For the general reason, write each point as the common anchor plus a vector in S. Expand a weighted combination of those points. The result separates into the sum of the weights times the anchor, plus a linear combination of vectors in S.",
    ("s12_affine", 17): "The second part stays in S because S is a subspace. The first part controls the location: how many copies of the anchor have we included? When the weights add to one, we retain precisely the one anchor that places us on the original translated set.",
    ("s12_affine", 18): "Weights summing to one preserve the affine set. Adding two points gives weights summing to two, which doubles the anchor and can leave that set. Weights summing to zero cancel the anchor altogether, leaving a direction in S. That is why differences between points reveal the underlying directions.",
    ("s10_matrices", 38): "Coordinates in a basis are unique, so adding two vectors adds their coordinate lists, and scaling a vector scales its coordinates. The coordinate map is linear. Apply that fact to each column and it becomes a matrix, with no need to memorise a conversion rule in advance.",
    ("s04_span", 20): "Notice what changed. Once the two moves lay on the same line, some destinations became impossible, and reachable destinations acquired many different recipes. The loss of a direction shows up both in the territory we can reach and in the settings that describe it.",
    ("s05_independence", 15): "We can now connect three descriptions of redundancy. A nontrivial combination gives zero. Some vector can be made from the others. And removing that vector leaves the reachable territory unchanged. These statements are equivalent. The picture suggests why; one short rearrangement makes the connection precise.",
    ("s05_independence", 16): "Start with a combination equal to zero in which at least one coefficient is nonzero. Pick a vector with a nonzero coefficient. We can solve for that vector using all the others. This is the step that turns a cancellation into evidence of redundancy.",
    ("s08_basis", 9): "A basis is an ordered list of vectors that spans the space and is linearly independent. Spanning means every destination has an address. Independence means no destination gets two different addresses. Both conditions matter: too few directions leave places unreachable, while redundant directions make the description ambiguous.",
    ("s08_basis", 10): "Here is the precise connection. A list is a basis exactly when every vector has one and only one representation using that list. This is worth proving, because the proof tells us why the condition about zero controls all the other destinations too.",
    ("s08_basis", 28): "How would you build a basis from a large collection of controls? Keep a direction when it expands the territory you can reach. Discard it when the directions already kept can reproduce it. Starting with a spanning list, this removes redundancy without losing a single destination.",
    ("s08_basis", 34): "And the survivors are independent. If a kept direction could be built from the earlier kept directions, we would have discarded it. Every kept direction added something new, and every discarded direction is still reproducible. That gives us both independence and spanning.",
    ("s08_basis", 35): "You can also start with independent directions and add more until they span the space. Each time you find a direction outside the current span, keep it. In our movement picture, attach another control only when it opens up new territory. Stop when the whole space is reachable.",
    ("s08_basis", 36): "Why does adding an outside direction preserve independence? A cancellation involving it with a nonzero coefficient would express it using the old directions. That contradicts being outside their span. Once the construction reaches everything, the resulting list is a basis.",
    ("s06_exchange", 9): "Insert the first new vector at the front. It already lies in the span of the old list, so the longer list has a redundancy. Scan from left to right for a vector that can be built from earlier ones. Replacing that vector preserves every destination the list could reach.",
    ("s06_exchange", 13): "Remove the second standard direction. We have just written it using the other vectors, so every old recipe can be rewritten without it. The first new direction has taken its place. The list still contains three vectors, and it still spans the whole three-dimensional space.",
    ("s06_exchange", 18): "The general result is called the exchange lemma. An independent list cannot be longer than a spanning list. One at a time, independent directions can replace old generators while preserving the span. Independence prevents a new direction from being redundant with the new directions already inserted, so each step must replace an old generator. There are only so many old generators to replace.",
    ("s08_basis", 40): "Now apply that counting result to two different bases of the same space. Each basis is independent and each spans. That combination forces their lengths to agree. The number is a property of the space, even though the arrows themselves can be chosen in many different ways.",
    ("s08_basis", 43): "Once the dimension is known, an independent list of that length is automatically a basis. So is a spanning list of that length. A list that already uses all the independent directions has no room to gain another; a spanning list of the right size has no room for redundancy.",
    ("s08_basis", 44): "To see why, extend an independent list to a basis. Since the list already has the dimension's number of vectors, nothing can be added. Or shrink a spanning list to a basis. The final size must be that same number, so nothing can be removed. The two requirements meet at exactly the basis size.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 7): "The counterexamples expose three different failures. The shifted line misses zero. The half-plane loses members when we reverse them. The union of the axes loses members when we add them. A set can pass one or two checks and still fail to be a subspace.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 8): "A subspace is a subset that remains a vector space under the same addition and scaling as the surrounding space. We don't have to recheck every algebraic identity from scratch. We need to check that our chosen world contains zero and that the operations keep us inside it.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 10): "Why do those checks suffice? The surrounding space already supplies identities such as commuting and regrouping sums. Those identities still hold for members of the smaller set. The question is whether adding and scaling those members produces another member of that set.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 11): "So the algebraic rules are inherited. The closure checks ensure we can actually perform the operations inside the subset. That distinction matters: a set might contain familiar vectors and still fail to contain their sums, as the two axes just showed.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 12): "Zero is present by the first check. And every member has its negative, because multiplying by minus one stays inside. We have kept both a home position and an undo for every move. Together with closure and the inherited identities, that gives a vector space.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 21): "A span is a subspace, and it is the smallest subspace containing its generators. This is what makes reachable territory such a useful picture. The generators force their scaled versions to be present, and addition forces every combination to be present too.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 24): "In finite dimensions, we can describe the same object as a subspace or as the span of a basis. Subspace emphasises the closed world of moves. Span emphasises the particular generators that build that world. Neither description requires us to use every coordinate direction in the ambient space.",
    ("s07_subspaces", 25): "Intersections also preserve this structure. The vectors that belong to each of two subspaces form a subspace of their own. Here two planes through the origin meet along a line through the origin. That shared line is their intersection.",
    ("s09_sums", 8): "Is the split unique? A vector on the line Y is t, zero, t. A vector in the plane U has the form a, a plus b, b. Equating them forces a and b both to equal t, but their sum must be zero. So t is zero. The two worlds share only the zero vector, which will be the key to uniqueness.",
    ("s09_sums", 9): "The sum of U and Y consists of all moves u plus w, where u belongs to U and w belongs to Y. It is the combined territory available when we can use either world's directions. It contains more than the union: adding a move from each can reach points belonging to neither world separately.",
    ("s09_sums", 10): "The sum is the smallest subspace containing both original subspaces. If one is generated by a list of u vectors and the other by a list of w vectors, the combined list generates their sum. All we have done is allow both sets of moves on the same control panel.",
    ("s09_sums", 14): "The sum is called direct when each destination has exactly one split into a piece from U and a piece from Y. Notice what is being claimed: uniqueness of the two component vectors. It does not require us to have already chosen coordinates for each component.",
    ("s09_sums", 15): "A direct sum is exactly a sum whose two subspaces share only zero. Overlap creates freedom to trade a shared move from one component to the other. Without a nonzero shared move, that trade is impossible, so the split is unique.",
    ("s09_sums", 18): "The dimension of the combined space is the dimension of U plus the dimension of Y, minus the dimension of their intersection. The shared directions were counted in both spaces, so we subtract their dimension once. Watch two overlapping planes make the formula visible.",
    ("s12_affine", 5): "An affine set is a translated subspace: an anchor point plus every vector in a subspace S. Its directions come from S; the anchor sets its location. If the anchor lies in S, translating changes nothing. A translate that genuinely misses the origin is affine but is not itself a linear subspace.",
    ("s12_affine", 10): "Subtract two points on the translated line. Both contain the same anchor, so it cancels. What remains is the difference of two vectors in S, which stays in S. You can also choose a different anchor on the line: its extra displacement is absorbed into S, leaving the same affine set.",
    ("s12_affine", 24): "Adding the bias shifts every output by the same vector. The image is now a translated subspace, an affine set. If the bias lies outside the original image, the translate misses the origin. If it already lies inside, the reachable set stays the same. The location can change without adding any new output direction.",
    ("s10_matrices", 3): "Each channel in this picture is one fixed vector, just like a movement control. Put those vectors side by side as the columns of a matrix V. The settings form a coefficient vector c, with one entry per column. Multiplying V by c produces their combined output.",
    ("s10_matrices", 6): "The shape tells us what goes in and what comes out. A matrix with n rows and k columns accepts k coefficients and produces n output coordinates. One coefficient per column, one output entry per row. This is the same movement machine, written compactly.",
    ("s10_matrices", 11): "The rank of V is the dimension of its column span. It counts the independent output directions the machine can produce. A basis can be extracted from the columns, so rank is also the largest number of independent columns. Having many columns does not guarantee a large rank.",
    ("s06_exchange", 28): "Take a weight matrix with seven hundred sixty-eight rows and three thousand seventy-two columns. Each column lives in a seven hundred sixty-eight dimensional output space. At most seven hundred sixty-eight columns can be independent. Thousands of input settings cannot create more independent output directions than the output space has room for.",
    ("s06_exchange", 29): "This limits independent directions, not the number of labels, concepts, or patterns a model can encode. Many distinct vectors can live in a low-dimensional space. What cannot happen is for more than the dimension's number of fixed directions to be linearly independent.",
    ("s06_exchange", 33): "This is the restriction used by low-rank adapters: the update has at most r independent output directions. It is a restriction on delta W. Adding that update to an existing matrix W does not force the combined matrix to have rank at most r. A small update space and a small total model space are different claims.",
    ("s11_inner_product", 2): "Picture the perpendicular shadow of v on the line through u. Its signed length measures how much of v points along that direction. The dot product is that signed length multiplied by the length of u. If u has unit length, the dot product equals the signed shadow length directly.",
    ("s11_inner_product", 8): "The Cauchy–Schwarz inequality says that the absolute dot product cannot exceed the product of the two lengths. Dividing by those lengths therefore produces a number between minus one and one, as a cosine must. Equality occurs when the two vectors are dependent. We will use the inequality here; the written chapter develops its proof.",
    ("s11_inner_product", 15): "For two nonzero vectors, divide the dot product by the product of their lengths. The resulting number lies between minus one and one. It is the cosine of their angle in this geometry. The division removes the effect of vector length so we can compare direction.",
    ("s11_inner_product", 32): "Why does overlap matter in a model? Imagine several signals being mixed and then read out by comparing the mixture with one signal direction. If the directions overlap, the readout picks up contributions from other signals. A dot product measures that overlap; it does not automatically recover the original coefficient.",
    ("s13_numerics", 24): "Now steer with two movement directions that are almost parallel. They are still independent, so every target in the plane remains reachable. But the geometry of the controls is about to make a particular target surprisingly difficult to describe with small settings.",
    ("s13_numerics", 33): "In computation, decide what reconstruction error you can accept. One useful rule allows a small absolute error plus an error proportional to the size of the target. Neither number should be hidden. The tolerance is part of what a numerical answer means.",
    ("s13_numerics", 48): "A small residual says the reconstruction is close to the target. It does not automatically say the recovered coefficients are close to the intended coefficients. Nearly dependent directions can turn very different recipes into almost the same destination. Keep reachability, uniqueness, and sensitivity separate when you interpret a solver's output.",
}
